ratfor - Rational Fortran

cmd/ratfor is the v7 ratfor (usr/src/cmd/ratfor), ported nearly
verbatim.  It reads Ratfor - the preprocessor language in Kernighan and
Plauger's "Software Tools" that adds if/else, while, for, repeat,
break, next, switch and define over plain Fortran - and emits FORTRAN
66.  The output is the same fixed-form cards a hand-written Fortran
program is, so the edit beside this directory (cmd/edit) keeps its
Ratfor source as .r files and is itself rebuilt from edit.c, the
reconstruction of that program.

The source is six hand-written files - r0.c (switch/case/default),
r1.c (loops, conditionals, break, next, goto), r2.c (the 72-column
output buffer), rio.c (input and the character classes), rlex.c (main,
the lexer, define and include) and rlook.c (the define hash table) -
plus the parser, generated from r.g by yacc.  The parser is committed
as y.tab.c because the cross build has no yacc to run before this
directory builds; r.g and y.tab.h are the source of the source.  The
one hand edit to y.tab.c is the yystack overflow test, split for c1 -
see the comment in yyparse.

Porting changes, all in the v6 spellings:

  - rio.c's type[] initializer was written without its '=' - restored.
  - rlex.c's FILE *outfil = { stdout } dropped the braces.
  - # include became #include.
  - built with -Dunix, which sets CONTFLD 1 (the continuation-card
    column); the v7 makefile relied on cc predefining unix, and this
    tree's ccc does not.
  - inclstat's fname is static: c1 cannot store a local array's address
    into a global (the curfile[infptr] = fname at its end).
  - y.tab.c's # line directives are stripped: pass0 does not read them.

The 2 shift/reduce conflicts yacc reports are v7's, not new; the
grammar settles them the way v7 meant.
